|~ `::doc`, the kind of a page, and what is declared under it: its
   constructor and the refusals of a payload that is no vector of
   segments, and the words that read a doc. ~|

:doc
  |~~ The kind of a doc, prose as a value, the value of a doc literal:
      text that carries quotes of qlang. It is how the language says what
      it is: every page is a doc, `doc` answers the page of a name, and
      because a doc is a value its code is checked and the next step can
      take it apart. A quote under `::link` is a link, a query a reader
      follows to another page, one under `::snippet` a query a reader
      runs to see what it shows, and a quote with no tag is code the
      prose holds. `content` answers its text, `segments` its parts,
      `links` its links and `snippets` its snippets; the laws of a name
      stand under it.
      ::snippet~(::doc | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals. ::link~(::link | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/hypertext | doc)
  ~~|

:doc/isThePageOfAName
  |~~ The page of a name is a doc. ~~|
  ::law~(::number/add | doc | type | eq ::doc)

:doc/splitsIntoSegments
  |~~ A doc of prose, code and prose has three segments. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 3)

:doc/buildsFromSegments
  |~~ Segments under `::doc` build the doc. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" ~(add 1)] | tag ::doc | eq (|~~a~(add 1)~~|))

:doc/construct
  |~~ The doc of a vector of prose strings and quotes. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:doc/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::doc` builds a doc from the vector of its segments, prose
      strings and quotes; a payload of another kind is refused.
  ~~|

:doc/PayloadNotVecError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::doc"x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::doc/PayloadNotVecError ::string])

:doc/ElementNotSegmentError
  |~~ Every element of a doc is a segment, a string of prose or a
      quote, and another value is refused at the index it stands at; a
      value is made prose by `print` first, `["a" (5 | print)] | tag ::doc`.
  ~~|

:doc/ElementNotSegmentError/refusesANumberAmongTheSegments
  |~~ A number among the segments is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" 1] | tag ::doc !| [type /index] | eq [::doc/ElementNotSegmentError 1])

:doc/content
  |~~ Answers the text of the doc, a string: its prose with each quote
      written where it stands, `|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | content` answering
      `" a ~(add 1) b "`, and a link or a snippet with its tags,
      `::link~(::vec | doc)`. The subject is a doc, so the page of a
      name is read by `doc` first, `::vec/join | doc | content`; a name
      declared without a page has none, `doc` answering null, which
      `content` refuses as it refuses a string. The parts of the doc in
      their order `segments` answers, and its links `links`.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::doc/segments | doc)
      ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:doc/content/answer
  |~~ The text of the doc, its quotes written where they stand. ~~|
  ::string

:doc/content/SubjectNotDocError
  |~~ `content` reads a doc: the page of a name is read by `doc` first,
      `::vec/join | doc | content`, and a name declared without a page
      has none, `doc` answering null.
  ~~|

:doc/content/writesEachQuoteWhereItStands
  |~~ The content writes each quote where it stands in the prose. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | content | eq " a ~(add 1) b ")

:doc/content/writesALinkWithItsTags
  |~~ A link is written with its tags. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ see ::link~(::vec | doc) ~~| | content | eq " see ::link~(::vec | doc) ")

:doc/content/readsThePageOfAWord
  |~~ The content of a word's page is its text. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/join | doc | content | contains "separator")

:doc/content/SubjectNotDocError/refusesTheNullOfANameWithoutAPage
  |~~ A name declared without a page has no content to read. ~~|
  ::law~(:x 1 | :x | doc | content
    !| [type /actualType] | eq [::doc/content/SubjectNotDocError ::null])

:doc/content/SubjectNotDocError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no doc. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | content !| type | eq ::doc/content/SubjectNotDocError)

:doc/segments
  |~~ Answers the parts of the doc in their order, a vector: each run of
      prose a string, and each span that reads as code a quote, bare or
      under the tags of its role, a link under `::link` and a snippet
      under `::snippet`, `|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments` answering
      `[" a " ~(add 1) " b "]`, each tag laid over the quote through its
      label as a tagged literal lays it, so a tag that takes no quote for
      its payload refuses the doc, `::number~(…)` answering
      `::number/PayloadNotNumberError`. A doc of prose alone answers a
      vector of one string.
      A span that does not read as code stays prose, so a quote left
      broken in a page is no link and no snippet. The segments are the
      payload the doc was built from, so `segments | tag ::doc` answers
      the doc again, and `content` writes them as one text. The subject
      is a doc, the page of a name read by `doc` first.
      ::link~(::doc/content | doc) ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
      ::link~(::doc/snippets | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:doc/segments/answer
  |~~ The prose and the quotes of the doc, in their order. ~~|
  ::vec

:doc/segments/SubjectNotDocError
  |~~ `segments` reads a doc, not its text: the page of a name is read
      by `doc` first, `::vec | doc | segments`.
  ~~|

:doc/segments/alternatesProseAndCode
  |~~ Prose and code alternate in the segments. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | segments | eq [" a " ~(add 1) " b "])

:doc/segments/keepsTheTagsOfALink
  |~~ A link stands among the segments under its tag. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ::link~(1) ~~| | segments | /1 | type | eq ::link)

:doc/segments/answersOneStringForProseAlone
  |~~ A doc of prose alone is one segment of prose. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ only prose ~~| | segments | eq [" only prose "])

:doc/segments/keepsABrokenQuoteInProse
  |~~ A span that does not read as code stays prose. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ~(1 | ) b ~~| | segments | count | eq 1)

:doc/segments/buildsTheDocAgainUnderItsTag
  |~~ The segments under `::doc` are the doc again. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | :d / | d | segments | tag ::doc | eq d)

:doc/segments/refusesARoleWhosePayloadIsNoQuote
  |~~ A quote under a tag that takes no quote refuses the doc. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ see ::number~(~(x)) ~~| !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/PayloadNotNumberError ::quote])

:doc/segments/SubjectNotDocError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no doc. ~~|
  ::law~("text" | segments !| [type /actualType] | eq [::doc/segments/SubjectNotDocError ::string])

:doc/links
  |~~ Answers the links of the doc in their order, a vector: each a quote
      under a stack of tags that holds `::link`, `::link~(::vec | doc)`
      or a host's `::Host::link~(…)`, the query a reader follows to
      another page. A doc without links answers the empty vector, and a
      snippet is no link. A link opens to what its query answers, so
      `::vec | doc | links | map ~open` reads every page the page of vectors
      names, in one query, and `parse` spells its query. The subject is
      a doc, the page of a name read by `doc` first.
      ::link~(::quote/open | doc) ::link~(::doc/snippets | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:doc/links/answer
  |~~ The links of the doc, each a query that answers another page. ~~|
  ::vec

:doc/links/SubjectNotDocError
  |~~ `links` reads a doc, not its text: the page of a name is read by
      `doc` first, `::vec | doc | links`.
  ~~|

:doc/links/findsTheLinkInProse
  |~~ A link inside prose is found. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ see ::link~(::vec | manifest) ~~| | links | count | eq 1)

:doc/links/findsALinkUnderAHostTag
  |~~ A link under a host's tag is a link. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ::Host::link~(1) b ~~| | links | count | eq 1)

:doc/links/leavesTheSnippetsOut
  |~~ A doc whose only quote is a snippet has no link. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ::snippet~(1) b ~~| | links | eq [])

:doc/links/runsWhereItLeads
  |~~ A link runs the query it holds. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ ::link~(::vec | manifest) ~~| | links | first
    | :l / | null | apply l | has ::vec/count)

:doc/links/SubjectNotDocError/refusesItsText
  |~~ The text of a page is no doc. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec | doc | content | links
    !| [type /actualType] | eq [::doc/links/SubjectNotDocError ::string])

:doc/snippets
  |~~ Answers the snippets of the doc in their order, a vector: each a
      quote under a stack of tags that holds `::snippet`, a query a
      reader runs to see what it shows, `::snippet~([2 1] | sort)`. A
      doc without snippets answers the empty vector, and a link is no
      snippet. A snippet opens to the answer it shows, so
      `snippets | map ~open` runs every example of a page. The subject is
      a doc, the page of a name read by `doc` first.
      ::link~(::quote/open | doc) ::link~(::doc/links | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:doc/snippets/answer
  |~~ The snippets of the doc, each a query a reader runs. ~~|
  ::vec

:doc/snippets/SubjectNotDocError
  |~~ `snippets` reads a doc, not its text: the page of a name is read
      by `doc` first, `::tag/manifest | doc | snippets`.
  ~~|

:doc/snippets/findsTheSnippetInProse
  |~~ A snippet inside prose is found. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ run ::snippet~([2 1] | sort) ~~| | snippets | count | eq 1)

:doc/snippets/leavesTheLinksOut
  |~~ A doc whose only quote is a link has no snippet. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ::link~(1) b ~~| | snippets | eq [])

:doc/snippets/opensToWhatItShows
  |~~ A snippet opens to what it shows. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ ::snippet~([2 1] | sort) ~~| | snippets | map ~open | eq [[1 2]])

:doc/snippets/SubjectNotDocError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no doc. ~~|
  ::law~("text" | snippets !| [type /actualType] | eq [::doc/snippets/SubjectNotDocError ::string])
